// typeset.typ implements one link rule — the underline, at "@s inline" — and
// neither link-print-url nor link-bare. Both are answers to a question a
// Typst document never asks: it composes for paper, so a URL that a reader
// needs on paper is written where it is wanted, and there is no screen
// rendering for an opt-out to differ from. All three links below therefore
// set identically, where the HTML file's second and third panes differ from
// its first.
The full argument runs on the project's own site, at
#link("https://example.org/measure")[example.org/measure].

The essay itself is online, at
#link("https://example.org/on-the-measure")[the measure of a line].

Corrections go to
#link("https://example.org/t/8f3d2x-corrections")[the correction form], whose
address is a tracking redirect nobody should copy by hand.
